# =============================================================================
# .env.example — Odal Node engine: the single source of truth for env vars.
# Copy and fill in:   cp .env.example .env
# .env is gitignored — never commit real secrets.
#
# Organised by deployable. The node (the MVP binary) reads the NODE section.
# The resolver is a separate deployable and reads the RESOLVER section from its
# own environment — the vars live here only so everything is in one place.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# NODE — the MVP binary `dpp-node` (vault + identity + integrator on one port,
# routed /vault/* /identity/* /integrator/*). This is what `odal up` runs.
# =============================================================================

# --- PostgreSQL (required) ---------------------------------------------------
# Two passwords, each defined ONCE. Docker Compose reads these to provision the
# Postgres container and build the connection URLs:
#   DATABASE_POSTGRES_PASS — superuser (container init + migrations)
#   DATABASE_APP_PASS      — odal_app role the node connects with
DATABASE_POSTGRES_PASS=dev_only_password
DATABASE_APP_PASS=dev_only_password
# Connection URLs the node binary reads directly. When running the node from
# source (dev), set these; the embedded passwords MUST match the two vars above.
# App role: no DDL; DELETE only on the tables ops/pg/README.md lists
# (counters and a job queue, never passport/audit/evidence). Single-tenant —
# isolation is an infrastructure boundary (one node per operator), not
# Row-Level Security. The node refuses to boot if this URL connects as a
# superuser: a superuser owns the audit table, so the append-only trigger
# cannot bind it and its guarantee would silently stop holding.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://odal_app:dev_only_password@localhost:5432/odal
# Privileged migration URL: if set, the node runs sqlx migrations at boot.
# Omit to manage migrations externally via `just migrate` / ops tooling.
DATABASE_MIGRATE_URL=postgres://postgres:dev_only_password@localhost:5432/odal

# --- Identity / signing key store (required) ---------------------------------
# Where the encrypted key store file is written. A filesystem path, not a
# secret — the node creates the file here, so a bare word puts your signing key
# in whatever directory the node happens to start from.
KEY_STORE_PATH=./dev-keystore.enc
# The passphrase protects the Ed25519 key every passport is signed with. Losing
# it makes published passports unverifiable; leaking it makes them forgeable.
# Generate one — do not reuse a value from a template:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
KEY_STORE_PASSPHRASE=
DID_WEB_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8001

# --- Local admin auth (needed once, so `odal bootstrap` can mint the first key)
# Basic-scheme credential with FULL ADMIN scope and no API-key row behind it, so
# it can also revoke every key (that is the lockout-recovery path). It is the
# most powerful credential this node accepts.
#
# Left UNSET on purpose: with either variable absent the local-admin provider is
# not constructed at all, so the node accepts no Basic credential. Set both only
# while bootstrapping, to values you generate, and unset them afterwards.
#
# The node REFUSES TO BOOT on the placeholder values that used to live here
# (`admin`/`admin`) — see `ensure_admin_credentials` in crates/dpp-node/src/config.rs.
# ADMIN_USERNAME=
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- Listener ----------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_PORT=8001
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated CORS origins. Empty = no CORS (server-side API-key access only).
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
# Event bus. Empty = NoOp (events discarded silently). Set to enable NATS
# JetStream — the node connects at boot and fails fast if it can't reach it.
NATS_URL=nats://localhost:4222
# Wasm sector-plugin directory.
PLUGINS_DIR=./plugins
# Concurrent rows during bulk import.
BATCH_CONCURRENCY=20
# Private Prometheus metrics endpoint (GET /metrics), served OFF the public API
# port. Loopback by default (127.0.0.1:9100); set a private interface to scrape
# remotely, or empty to disable. Leave unset for the default.
# METRICS_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9100
# Allow signed-webhook delivery to private/loopback addresses. Off by default:
# the SSRF guard refuses non-public receivers (and requires https). Set to true
# only on a self-hosted node whose receiver lives on your own internal network.
# WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE_TARGETS=false
# Hex-encoded Ed25519 public key to verify signed Wasm plugins. REQUIRED whenever
# PLUGINS_DIR contains plugins: the node now REFUSES TO BOOT with unsigned plugins
# present and no key (fail-closed). For local dev with unsigned plugins, set
# ALLOW_UNSIGNED_PLUGINS=true to opt out explicitly (never in production).
# PLUGIN_SIGNING_KEY=
# ALLOW_UNSIGNED_PLUGINS=false

# --- Compliance Current: the signed ruleset channel --------------------------
# Where signed ruleset bundles are dropped, and the base64url Ed25519 public key
# of the offline publisher that must have signed them. BOTH or NEITHER — a path
# without a pinned key configures no channel at all, because a node that adopts
# whatever bytes it is handed is worse than one on its baseline.
#
# Unset (the default) means the node runs its compiled-in baseline ruleset,
# versioned `baseline`. It never fails to boot over this.
#
# The node re-reads the channel at boot, on a timer, and whenever an admin calls
# POST /vault/api/v1/ruleset/reload (`odal ruleset reload`). Every adoption is
# fail-closed: a bundle whose signature or content hash does not check out, whose
# rules do not take effect yet, or that is older than the one already in force is
# refused and the ruleset in force keeps validating. Refusals increment
# ruleset_load_failures_total.
# RULESET_BUNDLE_PATH=
# RULESET_PUBLISHER_PUBKEY=
# How often to re-read the channel, in seconds. Default 300. Set 0 to poll never
# and leave the admin route as the only trigger.
# RULESET_POLL_INTERVAL_SECS=300

# =============================================================================
# RESOLVER — public QR / Digital-Link resolver `dpp-resolver`.
# A separate deployable (its own container, or a Cloudflare Worker) with its
# own environment. These vars are listed here only so everything is in one place;
# the node binary ignores them. Use RESOLVER_PORT to avoid collision with
# NODE_PORT when both are sourced from the same shell environment.
# =============================================================================
RESOLVER_PORT=8003
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379            # required by the resolver — response cache
VAULT_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8001/vault  # points at the node's vault sub-path
CACHE_TTL_SECS=30                           # worst-case recall-propagation window; raise only with that tradeoff in mind
RATE_LIMIT_RPM=120                          # per-IP request limit

# The public origin printed onto the product — the host a scanned QR code
# resolves against, baked into every passport's carrier URL at publish time.
#
# The default below is the project's own demo resolver and is not wired up yet.
# Every operator serves their own: set this to your resolver deployment's public
# origin before publishing anything real. It cannot be corrected afterwards —
# publishing starts the ESPR retention window and `odal passport archive`
# refuses inside it, so the labels would have to be reissued.
RESOLVER_BASE_URL=https://id.odal-node.io
# OPERATOR_DID_URL=                          # default: derived from VAULT_BASE_URL host

# Private Prometheus metrics endpoint (GET /metrics). Resolver default
# 127.0.0.1:9101 (distinct from the node's 9100 so both can run on one host).
# METRICS_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9101

# Scan telemetry (privacy-safe aggregate resolution counts). Off unless
# SCAN_INGEST_URL is set. The resolver accumulates counts in memory and flushes
# them to the node's internal, mTLS-gated ingest endpoint. SCAN_FLUSH_CLIENT_IDENTITY
# is a PEM bundle (private key + cert chain, CN=odal-resolver) presented for mTLS;
# without it the flush is plain HTTP (local dev only).
# SCAN_INGEST_URL=https://node.internal/vault/internal/scan-batch
# SCAN_FLUSH_INTERVAL_SECS=300
# SCAN_FLUSH_CLIENT_IDENTITY=/etc/odal/resolver-client.pem

# =============================================================================
# mTLS for the node's /vault/internal/* endpoints (scan ingest, and identity's
# internal sign/rotate when identity runs standalone). Trust is proxy-terminated:
# the terminating proxy verifies the client cert and forwards X-Client-Cert-*;
# MTLS_PROXY_SHARED_SECRET binds those headers to the proxy. Enforced by default;
# MTLS_ALLOW_INSECURE=true disables it for local dev / CI only.
# MTLS_PROXY_SHARED_SECRET=change-me
# MTLS_REQUIRED_ISSUER_CN=Odal Internal CA
# MTLS_ALLOW_INSECURE=false

# =============================================================================
# eIDAS qualified sealing — eID Easy Cloud Direct e-Sealing (CAdES).
#
# SEAL_PROVIDER unset (or `none`) means GhostSeal: published passports carry no
# seal, and NODE_PROFILE=production refuses to boot. `eideasy` is the only other
# value today; the variable exists so adding a QTSP later is not a config
# migration for every self-hoster. An unrecognised value fails the boot.
#
# Setting the SEAL_EIDEASY_* credentials WITHOUT selecting the provider is also
# refused — silently ghosting there would downgrade a node that was configured
# for qualified sealing into one that has none. Same reason a partial
# configuration fails, and why SEAL_EIDEASY_BASE_URL is allowlisted to the two
# hosts below.
#
# SEAL_EIDEASY_HMAC_KEY is shown once, when generated in eID Easy's Eseal
# Settings. Treat it like MTLS_PROXY_SHARED_SECRET: this file is mode 600.
# SEAL_CONFORMANCE_LEVEL is the baseline level every seal request asks for --
# B, T, LT or LTA -- and it defaults to LT. LT is the first level that stays
# verifiable after the signing certificate expires, which is what a passport
# needs, since its retention lock is permanent. An unrecognised value fails the
# boot rather than falling back to the default.
#
# THE TWO MUST AGREE. A request the backend does not advertise is refused before
# the (billable) call, so a node left on the default LT while its eID Easy client
# is enabled only for CAdES_BASELINE_T seals nothing: rows retry, then exhaust,
# and the passports stay published-but-unsealed. Either have eID Easy enable
# CAdES_BASELINE_LT for the client and leave the default alone, or set both
# variables to the same level deliberately.
#
# The local development sealer advertises B only -- it is self-signed with no
# timestamp -- so SEAL_PROVIDER=local additionally needs
# SEAL_CONFORMANCE_LEVEL=B.
# SEAL_PROVIDER=eideasy
# SEAL_EIDEASY_BASE_URL=https://test.eideasy.com     # sandbox; prod = https://id.eideasy.com
# SEAL_EIDEASY_CLIENT_ID=
# SEAL_EIDEASY_HMAC_KEY=
# SEAL_EIDEASY_SIGNATURE_PROFILE=CAdES_BASELINE_T    # optional; must be enabled for the client
# SEAL_CONFORMANCE_LEVEL=LT                          # optional; must match the profile above

# =============================================================================
# EU digital product passport registry (ESPR Art. 13).
#
# Unset means GhostRegistrySync: publish still works and registrations are
# enqueued, but nothing is submitted. Set both credentials to activate the
# sandbox adapter.
#
# The operator's legal name and country come from operator config (`odal
# operator`), not from here. Both land on the registration's operator
# identifier, and the registry rejects a registration carrying no legal name —
# a node with an unset legal name logs a warning at boot and every registration
# fails validation.
# EU_REGISTRY_CLIENT_ID=
# EU_REGISTRY_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Public base URL under which this deployment *serves* its continuity snapshots.
# Declared to the registry as each passport's independently-hosted back-up, as
# `<base>/<dppId>.json`.
#
# Deliberately separate from SNAPSHOT_S3_BUCKET: writing snapshots to object
# storage does not make them reachable. Until an operator states that they are
# served, no back-up link is declared — a URL the registry cannot fetch is worse
# than declaring none.
# SNAPSHOT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://backup.example.com/dpp
#
# Submit payloads that fail our own validation instead of refusing them. Off
# unless explicitly set, so the safe behaviour is the one you get by doing
# nothing. It exists because our local rules are an interpretation of the spec
# and may produce a false positive that should not need a code change to work
# around — not as a way to push known-bad records at a live registry.
# EU_REGISTRY_ALLOW_INVALID_PAYLOADS=false

# =============================================================================
# Ports: Postgres 5432 · NODE_PORT 8001 · RESOLVER_PORT 8003 · Redis 6379 · NATS 4222
#        Metrics (private): node 9100 · resolver 9101
